WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:02.820 --> 00:05.250
I'm Ingrid, a partner in our DC office.

2
00:05.640 --> 00:13.030
As you heard from other leaders, problem solving — or the process
by which we make decisions — is a core element of leadership.

3
00:13.480 --> 00:16.450
So, what does great problem solving look like?

4
00:17.030 --> 00:21.830
When the problem solving is okay,
you're probably getting to an answer to the problem.

5
00:22.280 --> 00:27.460
It may even be a reasonably good answer —
but you're not getting the best answer.

6
00:27.940 --> 00:35.200
And oftentimes what differentiates the okay versus the great
is whether you are leveraging the collective power of the group.

7
00:36.200 --> 00:43.230
Oftentimes one individual — in many cases
the leader of the group solving the problem —

8
00:43.560 --> 00:47.330
takes a disproportionate share
of the problem-solving burden.

9
00:47.810 --> 00:54.310
They may be imposing their own view on how to structure
the problem and go about the analysis.

10
00:54.820 --> 01:00.200
I think the great examples of problem solving are around
how you get the most out of everybody involved.

11
01:00.200 --> 01:05.670
And one of the tricks, as a problem-solving leader,
is to understand how to do that.

12
01:06.530 --> 01:10.910
If you're in an environment where you're working
through a problem with a number of people,

13
01:11.530 --> 01:16.640
it is probably more important to actually formalize
and put on a sheet of paper:

14
01:17.250 --> 01:23.840
What is our problem? What are the constraints?
Who are the stakeholders we need to align?

15
01:24.520 --> 01:32.830
And how are we structuring the problem to enable everybody
to eventually agree and align on the approach to get to the right solution?

16
01:33.290 --> 01:40.960
Conversely, if you're working through a problem and everybody's
saying the same things, it may even be the right answer.

17
01:41.380 --> 01:47.590
But in those cases, as a problem-solving leader,
you want to think about how you can push that thinking.

18
01:48.100 --> 01:54.200
Do you want to introduce the concept
of a red team and a blue team?

19
01:54.200 --> 02:02.010
Do you want to appoint somebody — even yourself — as a devil's advocate,
to really test whether the idea you're all thinking is the right or best answer?

20
02:02.370 --> 02:12.070
Being a collaborative problem-solving leader is great not only to get
to the right answer, the best answer one can, or the answer as quickly as possible.

21
02:12.450 --> 02:19.780
It has a second benefit, which is the impact of the collaboration
and getting everybody really bought into that solution.
